1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:02,724
AI agents can propose production changes

2
00:00:02,724 --> 00:00:05,552
faster than teams can safely review them.

3
00:00:05,552 --> 00:00:08,380
For an online database migration, the dangerous

4
00:00:08,380 --> 00:00:11,613
question is not whether each step is valid.

5
00:00:11,613 --> 00:00:15,249
It is whether one compatibility-window ordering corrupts the result.

6
00:00:15,249 --> 00:00:18,481
CutoverProof puts that change on trial before production.

7
00:00:19,281 --> 00:00:22,167
CutoverProof is a shared change contract.

8
00:00:22,167 --> 00:00:25,535
The agent prepares the case, PostgreSQL produces

9
00:00:25,535 --> 00:00:28,902
independent evidence, and the engineer keeps authority.

10
00:00:28,902 --> 00:00:33,713
In this signed-in workspace, five WebMCP browser tools are ready.

11
00:00:34,513 --> 00:00:38,395
The agent can list and inspect migration contracts, prepare a

12
00:00:38,395 --> 00:00:42,665
visible review, read verified evidence, and open a pending repair review.

13
00:00:42,665 --> 00:00:46,547
Every tool has a closed schema and explicit safety hints.

14
00:00:46,547 --> 00:00:49,653
There is no tool to run arbitrary SQL,

15
00:00:49,653 --> 00:00:52,759
decide the verdict, deploy, or approve a repair.

16
00:00:53,559 --> 00:00:57,085
I ask the browser agent to inspect the status-normalization

17
00:00:57,085 --> 00:01:01,003
contract and focus on stale writes during the compatibility window.

18
00:01:01,003 --> 00:01:04,530
Its only write action creates this idempotent review draft.

19
00:01:04,530 --> 00:01:07,273
The card appears in my workspace and

20
00:01:07,273 --> 00:01:10,015
states the boundary clearly: nothing has executed.

21
00:01:10,815 --> 00:01:14,235
The agent hands the decision back to me.

22
00:01:14,235 --> 00:01:16,800
I inspect the migration objective, candidate

23
00:01:16,800 --> 00:01:19,793
budget, operations, invariant count, and isolated environment.

24
00:01:19,793 --> 00:01:23,641
Only this visible human control can start the assessment.

25
00:01:23,641 --> 00:01:27,061
The internal planner may choose experiments, but it

26
00:01:27,061 --> 00:01:30,481
still cannot cross the execution boundary by itself.

27
00:01:31,281 --> 00:01:36,224
A dangerous ordering is executed against a freshly reset PostgreSQL sandbox.

28
00:01:36,224 --> 00:01:39,819
Here, an old payment write changes the legacy

29
00:01:39,819 --> 00:01:43,864
status after backfill, leaving the new status reference stale.

30
00:01:43,864 --> 00:01:46,560
A read-only invariant returns the exact

31
00:01:46,560 --> 00:01:49,257
violating row, so CutoverProof blocks cutover.

32
00:01:49,257 --> 00:01:53,751
The model proposed the experiment; deterministic code decided what happened.

33
00:01:54,551 --> 00:01:57,154
WebMCP can read the verified evidence

34
00:01:57,154 --> 00:01:59,758
and navigate to this repair decision.

35
00:01:59,758 --> 00:02:01,493
It cannot approve it.

36
00:02:01,493 --> 00:02:04,531
A named engineer authorizes one allow-listed sandbox

37
00:02:04,531 --> 00:02:08,002
repair, and CutoverProof replays the identical failing schedule.

38
00:02:08,002 --> 00:02:11,039
The final record connects the counterexample, human

39
00:02:11,039 --> 00:02:14,511
approval, and passing replay without claiming exhaustive safety.

40
00:02:15,311 --> 00:02:22,519
CutoverProof starts with PostgreSQL migrations, but the ambition is broader: every agent-led production change should have

41
00:02:22,519 --> 00:02:29,727
a declared contract, bounded experiments, independent proof, and a human authority line the agent cannot cross.
